---
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import DemoLayout from '../../layouts/DemoLayout.astro';

const numbers: ReadonlyArray<number> = Array.from({ length: 100 }, (_: unknown, index: number): number => index + 1);

const listExample: string = `<ol>
    <li></li>
    <li></li>
    <li></li>
    <!-- … and so on, up to 100 -->
</ol>`;

const counterExample: string = `ol { counter-reset: number; }
li { counter-increment: number; }`;

const numberExample: string = `li:not(:nth-child(3n)):not(:nth-child(5n))::after {
    content: counter(number);
}`;

const fizzExample: string = `li:nth-child(3n)::before {
    content: "Fizz";
}`;

const buzzExample: string = `li:nth-child(5n)::after {
    content: "Buzz";
}`;

const colourExample: string = `li:nth-child(3n)  { background: var(--brand-rose); }
li:nth-child(5n)  { background: var(--brand-blue); }
li:nth-child(15n) { background: var(--brand-violet); }`;
---
<DemoLayout path="/Demos/FizzBuzz">
    <Fragment slot="what">
        <p>
            FizzBuzz began as a counting game for children. Players count upwards in turn, saying "fizz" instead of any
            number divisible by 3, "buzz" instead of any number divisible by 5, and "fizzbuzz" for numbers divisible by
            both. It became a famous first test in programming interviews, because solving it takes a loop, a counter and
            a couple of decisions: the basics of programming in miniature.
        </p>
        <p>The list below is a hundred empty list items. Everything you can read in it was put there by CSS.</p>
    </Fragment>

    <ol slot="demo" class="fizzbuzz" role="list" aria-label="FizzBuzz from 1 to 100">
        {numbers.map(() => <li></li>)}
    </ol>

    <Fragment slot="how">
        <p>
            The HTML is an ordered list, <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>, of a hundred list items, <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>, with
            nothing in any of them. The CSS has to work out each item's number, test what that number is divisible by, and
            write the answer. It does it without ever dividing, using three features: a counter, the
            <code>:nth-child()</code> selector, and generated content.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">The HTML</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{listExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>Counting</h3>
        <p>
            A CSS counter is a number the browser keeps while it reads through the page. The list sets one called
            <code>number</code> to 0, and every item adds 1 to it, so the first item's number is 1, the second's is 2, and so
            on. The HTML never says which item is which.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">Counting source</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{counterExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>Testing for divisibility</h3>
        <p>
            <code>:nth-child(3n)</code> matches every third child of its parent. The <code>n</code> stands for every whole
            number in turn, so <code>3n</code> is 3, 6, 9, 12 and so on: exactly the numbers divisible by 3. Matching it is
            the same as asking whether the item's number leaves no remainder when divided by 3, which most programming
            languages write as <code>number % 3 == 0</code>. <code>:nth-child(5n)</code> asks the same about 5.
        </p>

        <h3>Plain numbers</h3>
        <p>
            Items divisible by neither should show their number. <code>:not()</code> turns a test round, so
            <code>:not(:nth-child(3n))</code> matches items that aren't divisible by 3. Writing two of them together means
            both must hold: not divisible by 3, and not divisible by 5. Those items get an <code>::after</code>, a piece of
            content that CSS adds at the end of an element, holding the counter's value.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">Plain numbers source</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{numberExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>Fizz</h3>
        <p>
            Items divisible by 3 get the word "Fizz" in their <code>::before</code>, the content CSS adds at the start of an
            element.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">Fizz source</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{fizzExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>Buzz</h3>
        <p>
            Items divisible by 5 get the word "Buzz" in their <code>::after</code>. It's free for them to use, because the
            plain numbers rule leaves out anything divisible by 5.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">Buzz source</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{buzzExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>FizzBuzz</h3>
        <p>
            There's no rule for FizzBuzz, because it doesn't need one. A number divisible by both 3 and 5, such as 15, 30 or
            45, matches the Fizz rule and the Buzz rule, so it gets both. Fizz is at the start of the element and Buzz is at
            the end, with nothing in between, so together they read "FizzBuzz". That's why one uses <code>::before</code> and
            the other <code>::after</code>: two rules writing to the same place would overwrite each other.
        </p>
        <p>
            The colours do need a rule each, because an element has only one background. Multiples of 3 are rose and multiples
            of 5 are blue; a multiple of 15 is both, so a last rule, which wins because it comes last, makes it violet.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">Colours source</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{colourExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>The end result</h3>
        <p>
            Every item divisible by neither 3 nor 5 shows its number. Every item divisible by 3 starts with Fizz, every item
            divisible by 5 ends with Buzz, and every item divisible by both says FizzBuzz. The list here has a hundred items,
            but the rules work for a list of any length.
        </p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="why">
        <p>
            If FizzBuzz is a fair first test of a programmer, it's a fair first test of a language. CSS has everything it
            needs: the counter counts, <code>:nth-child()</code> tests for divisibility, and the selectors choose what to
            show. There's no loop to write, because the browser already applies every rule to every item.
        </p>
        <p>
            What the CSS can't do is make the list longer. The rules would carry on for ever, but the HTML decides how far
            they count.
        </p>
    </Fragment>
</DemoLayout>

<style>
    .fizzbuzz {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(6.5rem, 1fr));
        gap: 0.5rem;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        counter-reset: number;
    }

    .fizzbuzz li {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        min-height: 2.75rem;
        padding-inline: 0.25rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
        background: var(--surface-alt);
        color: var(--text-muted);
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
        counter-increment: number;
    }

    .fizzbuzz li:not(:nth-child(3n)):not(:nth-child(5n))::after {
        content: counter(number);
    }

    .fizzbuzz li:nth-child(3n)::before {
        content: 'Fizz';
    }

    .fizzbuzz li:nth-child(5n)::after {
        content: 'Buzz';
    }

    .fizzbuzz li:nth-child(3n),
    .fizzbuzz li:nth-child(5n) {
        color: #000;
        font-weight: 600;
    }

    .fizzbuzz li:nth-child(3n) {
        background: var(--brand-rose);
    }

    /* Blue is the one colour here that reads better under white text than black. */
    .fizzbuzz li:nth-child(5n) {
        background: var(--brand-blue);
        color: #fff;
    }

    .fizzbuzz li:nth-child(15n) {
        background: var(--brand-violet);
        color: #000;
    }
</style>
